    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d93-a2">

                <pb facs="0231" n="159" sameAs="frus:frus1882/pg159"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 2 in No.
                    308.—Translation.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Dr. Busch to Mr.
                            Everett.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Foreign Office</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1882-03-22"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">March</hi> 22,
                    1882</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>The undersigned has the honor to inform Mr. H. Sidney Everett, charge d’affaires
                    of the United States of America, in reply to the esteemed note of the 24th
                    ultimo, concerning a consignment of American pork which has been detained at
                    Altona, that the prohibition of June 25, 1880, is still in force and is
                    especially applicable to prepared pigs’ tongues.</p>
                <p>After a careful examination, in the proper quarter, of the facts in the case, it
                    has not been considered feasible to make an exception to the existing
                    prohibition in favor of the large consignment of pigs’ tongues and the
                    preparation known as “brawn” referred to in the esteemed note.</p>
                <p>The undersigned avails, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>BUSCH.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
